{"status": "claim_result", "index": 1, "result": {"claim": "The James Webb Space Telescope orbits the Sun near the second Lagrange point.", "evidence": "Evidence 1: L2 is one of five Sun-Earth Lagrange points, positions in space where the gravitational pull of the Sun and Earth combine such...\n\nEvidence 2: Jul 22, 2026 \u00b7 L2 is the second Sun-Earth Lagrange point, 1.5 million km beyond Earth. Learn why it's the perfect cold, dark home for telescopes like JWST. On 22 January 2022, Webb successfully completed a final correction burn that placed it into its desired orbit, roughly 1.5 million kilometers away from the Earth at what is called the second Sun-Earth Lagrange point, or \u201cL2\u201d for short. 1 day ago \u00b7 JWST orbits the Sun at the second Lagrange point (L2), approximately 1.5 million kilometres from Earth in the anti-sunward direction. It follows a halo orbit around L2 rather than orbiting Earth directly. Jan 24, 2022 \u00b7 After launch, the James Webb Space Telescope will travel to its orbital destination. Webb will perform its science mission while orbiting a location in space, called the second Lagrange point, or L2 for short. L2 is located one million miles from Earth.", "source_url": "https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/13553, https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/webbs-orbit-at-sun-earth-lagrange-point-2-l2/", "verification": {"label": "supported", "confidence": 0.9857083559036255, "probabilities": [0.0013329159701243043, 0.012958738021552563, 0.9857083559036255], "logits": [-2.9125, -0.6381, 3.6935], "evidence_scores": {"decided_by": 1, "snippets": [{"source": "web", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/webbs-orbit-at-sun-earth-lagrange-point-2-l2/", "label": "unknown", "probabilities": [0.0039924876764416695, 0.9941222071647644, 0.00188532960601151], "logits": [-1.399, 4.1184, -2.1493], "decisive": true}, {"source": "web", "url": "https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/13553", "label": "supported", "probabilities": [0.0013329159701243043, 0.01295873802155